1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:02,095
These men are laughing at an emperor.

2
00:00:02,965 --> 00:00:06,865
They're Cossacks, and they're writing
to the Sultan of the Ottoman Empire,

3
00:00:06,885 --> 00:00:08,899
one of the most powerful men on earth.

4
00:00:09,509 --> 00:00:11,569
Every line is an insult.

5
00:00:11,589 --> 00:00:13,769
Some are too filthy to read out here.

6
00:00:14,509 --> 00:00:16,329
The letter may never have existed.

7
00:00:16,349 --> 00:00:17,599
But the men did.

8
00:00:18,140 --> 00:00:23,960
Almost every face belongs to a real person:
a general, a historian, an opera star,

9
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:25,230
a coachman.

10
00:00:25,874 --> 00:00:28,414
It took Ilya Repin eleven years.

11
00:00:28,434 --> 00:00:31,787
And when it was done, another
emperor paid a fortune for it.

12
00:00:33,887 --> 00:00:35,547
The story goes like this.

13
00:00:35,567 --> 00:00:39,307
Sometime in the 1670s,
Sultan Mehmed the Fourth

14
00:00:39,327 --> 00:00:43,947
sends a letter to the Zaporozhian
Cossacks, a free army of warriors

15
00:00:43,967 --> 00:00:47,856
living beyond the rapids of the
Dnieper River, in what is now Ukraine.

16
00:00:48,756 --> 00:00:53,376
Runaway peasants, adventurers,
men with no master.

17
00:00:53,396 --> 00:00:55,296
They elected their own leaders,

18
00:00:55,316 --> 00:00:59,059
and they spent their lives fighting
the Ottomans and the Crimean Tatars.

19
00:00:59,669 --> 00:01:02,769
Mehmed had been sultan
since the age of six.

20
00:01:02,789 --> 00:01:06,289
Under him, the empire reached
deeper into Europe than ever,

21
00:01:06,309 --> 00:01:10,106
and a few years later, his army
would be at the gates of Vienna.

22
00:01:10,846 --> 00:01:12,586
He lists his titles.

23
00:01:12,606 --> 00:01:14,426
Brother of the sun and moon.

24
00:01:14,446 --> 00:01:16,666
Lord of kingdom after kingdom.

25
00:01:16,686 --> 00:01:21,411
And he orders them to surrender,
voluntarily and without any resistance.

26
00:01:22,411 --> 00:01:24,311
The Cossacks write back.

27
00:01:24,331 --> 00:01:28,231
They call him a Babylonian
scullion, a Macedonian wheelwright,

28
00:01:28,251 --> 00:01:32,184
a brewer of Jerusalem, and
things that are much, much worse.

29
00:01:32,924 --> 00:01:37,304
They explain that they can't date the
letter, because they don't own a calendar.

30
00:01:37,324 --> 00:01:41,094
And they sign off with an invitation
we'll leave to your imagination.

31
00:01:42,094 --> 00:01:43,674
Did any of this happen?

32
00:01:43,694 --> 00:01:45,354
Probably not as written.

33
00:01:45,374 --> 00:01:49,964
Many versions survive, with different
dates, different signatures,

34
00:01:50,014 --> 00:01:51,607
even different Cossacks.

35
00:01:52,087 --> 00:01:57,027
Historians think it began as a pamphlet,
one of many fake letters from the sultan

36
00:01:57,047 --> 00:01:58,867
that went around Europe.

37
00:01:58,887 --> 00:02:03,267
But two centuries later, people
were still copying it out by hand.

38
00:02:03,287 --> 00:02:07,181
In Ukraine, Repin said, every
church sexton had a copy.

39
00:02:09,081 --> 00:02:13,773
In the summer of 1878, Repin
is a guest at Abramtsevo,

40
00:02:13,801 --> 00:02:19,018
the country estate of Savva Mamontov, a
railway tycoon who loves to collect artists.

41
00:02:19,758 --> 00:02:22,298
Repin had grown up with these stories.

42
00:02:22,318 --> 00:02:25,178
He was born in Chuhuiv, in Ukraine,

43
00:02:25,198 --> 00:02:29,818
and as a young man he had illustrated
Gogol's Cossack novel, Taras Bulba.

44
00:02:29,838 --> 00:02:32,378
Gogol wrote about them
for good reason, he said.

45
00:02:32,398 --> 00:02:33,648
It's all true.

46
00:02:34,296 --> 00:02:38,116
Russia has just fought another
war against the Ottoman Empire.

47
00:02:38,136 --> 00:02:42,783
And the story goes that one evening, someone
reads the old Cossack letter out loud.

48
00:02:43,573 --> 00:02:45,183
Repin can't let it go.

49
00:02:45,203 --> 00:02:48,703
Before the summer is over, he
has made a first pencil sketch:

50
00:02:48,723 --> 00:02:51,157
Cossacks around a table, laughing.

51
00:02:52,157 --> 00:02:55,897
Two years later, he goes to
see their land for himself.

52
00:02:55,917 --> 00:02:59,577
He takes his fifteen-year-old
pupil, Valentin Serov,

53
00:02:59,597 --> 00:03:01,531
who will become a great painter too.

54
00:03:02,141 --> 00:03:07,081
They travel along the Dnieper, past
the rapids, to Khortytsia island,

55
00:03:07,101 --> 00:03:09,323
the old heartland of the Cossacks.

56
00:03:09,803 --> 00:03:15,673
Repin draws everything: faces in
the villages, old weapons, clothes.

57
00:03:15,723 --> 00:03:17,783
A collector, Vasyl Tarnovsky,

58
00:03:17,803 --> 00:03:20,952
opens his whole collection of
Cossack antiquities to him.

59
00:03:21,692 --> 00:03:25,512
That autumn, Leo Tolstoy
comes to his studio in Moscow

60
00:03:25,532 --> 00:03:27,352
and tells him to think bigger.

61
00:03:27,372 --> 00:03:31,112
Not a funny little episode, but
the spirit of a whole people.

62
00:03:31,132 --> 00:03:34,450
Repin makes the canvas
wider, and adds more men.

63
00:03:35,190 --> 00:03:37,810
Repin is already in
love with his Cossacks.

64
00:03:37,830 --> 00:03:42,850
He writes to his friend, the critic
Vladimir Stasov: No one on this earth

65
00:03:42,870 --> 00:03:47,024
has felt liberty, equality and
fraternity as deeply as they!

66
00:03:48,924 --> 00:03:50,984
Here is the secret of this painting.

67
00:03:51,004 --> 00:03:53,594
Repin didn't invent these Cossacks.

68
00:03:53,724 --> 00:03:55,095
He cast them.

69
00:03:56,095 --> 00:04:00,632
The scribe in the middle, grinning
as he writes, is Dmytro Yavornytsky,

70
00:04:00,655 --> 00:04:04,277
a young historian of the Cossacks
who became Repin's adviser.

71
00:04:04,757 --> 00:04:06,097
He didn't want to pose.

72
00:04:06,117 --> 00:04:11,662
So Repin handed him a magazine of cartoons,
waited for the smile, and painted it.

73
00:04:12,532 --> 00:04:16,832
The old ataman leaning over him with
a pipe, the legendary Ivan Sirko,

74
00:04:16,852 --> 00:04:21,632
is a real general: Mikhail Dragomirov,
commander of the army in Kyiv.

75
00:04:21,652 --> 00:04:25,864
When Repin met him, he is said
to have cried: a living Sirko!

76
00:04:26,604 --> 00:04:29,064
Dragomirov was famous for his wit.

77
00:04:29,084 --> 00:04:32,184
The story goes that he once
forgot the tsar's name day,

78
00:04:32,204 --> 00:04:35,784
and sent his congratulations
two days late, by telegram:

79
00:04:35,804 --> 00:04:39,687
for the third day we've been
drinking to Your Majesty's health.

80
00:04:40,687 --> 00:04:43,867
And somewhere in this
crowd is Fyodor Stravinsky,

81
00:04:43,887 --> 00:04:47,467
a star of the opera in St Petersburg,
and the father of the composer

82
00:04:47,487 --> 00:04:48,963
Igor Stravinsky.

83
00:04:49,833 --> 00:04:53,013
The hardest model to get was a bald head.

84
00:04:53,033 --> 00:04:55,813
Repin wanted the back
of one particular skull,

85
00:04:55,833 --> 00:05:00,611
belonging to an imperial official,
Georgy Alekseyev, who refused to sit.

86
00:05:01,091 --> 00:05:05,791
So Yavornytsky invited him to breakfast
and brought out his coin collection.

87
00:05:05,811 --> 00:05:11,471
While Alekseyev bent over the coins,
Repin quietly drew the back of his head.

88
00:05:11,491 --> 00:05:15,597
It takes centuries, he supposedly
said, to make a head like that.

89
00:05:16,597 --> 00:05:18,977
The collector Tarnovsky became the judge.

90
00:05:18,997 --> 00:05:21,617
His coachman became a ragged warrior.

91
00:05:21,637 --> 00:05:27,437
A professor, a teacher, a student, even
a stranger Repin met on a river pier.

92
00:05:29,337 --> 00:05:31,237
Then Repin got stuck.

93
00:05:31,257 --> 00:05:35,832
Again and again, he put the Cossacks
aside and painted something else.

94
00:05:36,312 --> 00:05:37,812
A religious procession.

95
00:05:37,832 --> 00:05:40,102
A saint stopping an execution.

96
00:05:40,232 --> 00:05:45,819
And in 1885, Ivan the Terrible, cradling
the son he has just struck down.

97
00:05:46,689 --> 00:05:51,149
Perhaps the darkest picture he ever
painted, made between the laughs.

98
00:05:51,169 --> 00:05:53,309
The tsar banned it from exhibition.

99
00:05:53,329 --> 00:05:55,823
We told that story in a
Short on this channel.

100
00:05:56,563 --> 00:05:59,103
But Repin always came
back to the Cossacks.

101
00:05:59,123 --> 00:06:03,663
In 1888, he went all the way
to the Kuban, in the Caucasus,

102
00:06:03,683 --> 00:06:07,724
where descendants of the Zaporozhians
still lived, to draw their faces.

103
00:06:08,464 --> 00:06:11,404
In 1891, it was finally done.

104
00:06:11,424 --> 00:06:14,524
Over two metres high,
three and a half wide.

105
00:06:14,544 --> 00:06:19,964
And along the bottom edge, next to his
name, Repin wrote the years it had cost him:

106
00:06:19,984 --> 00:06:22,014
1880 to 1891.

107
00:06:24,064 --> 00:06:25,804
Not everyone laughed.

108
00:06:25,824 --> 00:06:29,804
The critic Alexandre Benois
called it coarse and ridiculous.

109
00:06:29,824 --> 00:06:32,135
Others said it had no ideals at all.

110
00:06:32,875 --> 00:06:38,135
But one man liked it enough to pay
thirty-five thousand rubles, an enormous sum:

111
00:06:38,155 --> 00:06:41,155
Alexander the Third,
Emperor of all Russia.

112
00:06:42,025 --> 00:06:43,205
Think about that.

113
00:06:43,225 --> 00:06:49,045
A painting of free men mocking an emperor
went to hang in an emperor's palace.

114
00:06:49,065 --> 00:06:50,805
More than a century earlier,

115
00:06:50,825 --> 00:06:55,045
Catherine the Great's army had destroyed
the last fortress of the Cossacks.

116
00:06:55,065 --> 00:06:58,417
Now her great-great-grandson
owned their portrait.

117
00:06:59,417 --> 00:07:03,935
Repin wrote to Yavornytsky with
the news, and one word: hurrah!

118
00:07:04,675 --> 00:07:08,335
With the money, he bought a
country estate near Vitebsk,

119
00:07:08,355 --> 00:07:10,835
where he would spend
his summers for years.

120
00:07:11,315 --> 00:07:14,415
The painting won gold medals
in Munich and Budapest.

121
00:07:14,435 --> 00:07:18,175
And in 1897, the next
tsar, Nicholas the Second,

122
00:07:18,195 --> 00:07:21,980
gave it to a new museum in St
Petersburg: the Russian Museum.

123
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:25,700
But Repin wasn't finished with them.

124
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:30,056
He had already started a second version,
meant to be more historically accurate.

125
00:07:30,536 --> 00:07:33,636
Look closely, and you
can spot the differences.

126
00:07:33,656 --> 00:07:38,715
The scribe is older now, and he peers at
the letter through little round spectacles.

127
00:07:39,455 --> 00:07:42,395
That version ended up
in Kharkiv, in Ukraine,

128
00:07:42,415 --> 00:07:45,195
not far from the town
where Repin was born.

129
00:07:45,215 --> 00:07:50,845
In 1907, Repin gave the same museum
his portrait of General Dragomirov,

130
00:07:50,975 --> 00:07:52,308
his living Sirko.

131
00:07:53,048 --> 00:07:57,668
In March 2022, Russian shelling
shattered the museum's windows.

132
00:07:57,688 --> 00:08:01,744
Its staff took the second Cossacks
off the wall and moved it to safety.

133
00:08:02,484 --> 00:08:06,384
And this June, a Russian
drone set the museum on fire.

134
00:08:06,404 --> 00:08:08,557
The second Cossacks survived.

135
00:08:10,457 --> 00:08:15,157
Years later, at a dinner in his honour,
Yavornytsky raised a glass to Repin.

136
00:08:15,177 --> 00:08:17,637
He thanked him for making
the Cossacks famous.

137
00:08:17,657 --> 00:08:20,437
We were small, he said,
close to the ground.

138
00:08:20,457 --> 00:08:22,182
Now we stand a head taller.

139
00:08:23,182 --> 00:08:28,602
Since 2014, Ukrainian soldiers have
restaged this painting again and again,

140
00:08:28,622 --> 00:08:31,630
in uniform, writing replies of their own.

141
00:08:32,370 --> 00:08:35,550
So, did the Cossacks
really write to the sultan?

142
00:08:35,570 --> 00:08:37,209
Probably not like this.

143
00:08:37,949 --> 00:08:40,169
But Repin wasn't painting a document.

144
00:08:40,189 --> 00:08:42,009
He was painting a laugh.

145
00:08:42,029 --> 00:08:46,174
Free men, laughing at an
empire, and meaning every word.

146
00:08:47,174 --> 00:08:49,394
The letter may never have been sent.

147
00:08:49,414 --> 00:08:51,139
It's still being written.

148
00:08:52,919 --> 00:08:56,363
It hangs in the State Russian
Museum, in St Petersburg.
